spark has a habit of creating the plan form the beginning if we are using any action.
  By using cache() function it helps in temporary storage of the previous partitions and spill the rest to the disk.
its a special kind of persist which uses the ram and whenits full it pushes the rest of the partitions to the disk

persist: persist helps in persisting the partition into the memory.

Types of memory level in persist:

Memory_only: df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY)
--------------------------------------------------
this works for RDD's only where the partition are stored in a deserialised jvm objects.
its faster as it is only reading the RAM
if the RAM is failing to store the partition, spark drops the rest and recaluclate it as per requirement
It is default for RDD's

Memory and Disk: df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) / df.cache()
----------------------------------------------------------
This works for dataframe and the partitions are stored as per requirement via spark in RAM
Its a little bit slow if the jobs are using the partition from disk to invoke any action
its safer than memory only as rest of the partitions are not dropped


Total types of storage level:
-----------------------------
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) used for RDD's only
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) used for dataframes
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY_2) which saves the data twice
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY_SER) not available in spark
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK_SER) not available in spark

